<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Directional derivatives-number 7</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Find the direction in which the maximum rate of change of the equation occurs and the maximum rate of change for the function:</p>
<p>\(f(x,y,z)={@quiz_expr@}\) at \(({@point_x@},{@point_y@},{@point_z@})\)</p>
<p><b>(give your answers in \(1\) decimal place)</b></p>
<p><b>Step \(1\):</b> Differentiate partially \(f \)with respect to \(x, y\) and \(z\) respectively. </p>
<br>
<p>\(\nabla f_x=\)[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]][[feedback:prt1]]</p>
<p>\(\nabla f_y=\)[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]][[feedback:prt2]]</p>
<p>\(\nabla f_z=\)[[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]][[feedback:prt3]]</p>
<br>
<p><b>Step \(2\):</b>  Now, find the gradient  at the points \(({@point_x@},{@point_y@},{@point_z@})\) using the gradient functions you have found in Step 1 above</p></p>
<p> (Hint: Substitute for \(x,y\) \(z\) in the gradient functions above)</p>
<p>\(\nabla f_x=\)[[input:ans4]]<p>
<p> [[validation:ans4]][[feedback:prt4]]</p>

<p>\(\nabla f_y=\)[[input:ans5]]</p>
<p> [[validation:ans5]][[feedback:prt5]]</p>

<p>\(\nabla f_z=\)[[input:ans6]] </p>
<p>[[validation:ans6]][[feedback:prt6]]</p>
<br>
<p><b>Step \(3\):</b>  Determine the direction in which the maximum rate of change of the function occurs.</p>
<p>\(\nabla f_x(x-x_1)+\nabla f_y(y-y_1)+\nabla f_z(z-z_1)=\) \( ( \) [[input:ans7]] ,[[input:ans8]] ,[[input:ans9]] \( ) \) </p>
<p> [[validation:ans7]][[feedback:prt7]]  [[validation:ans8]][[feedback:prt8]]  [[validation:ans9]][[feedback:prt9]]</p>
<br>
<p><b>Step \(4\):</b> Deduce the maximum rate of change  of \(f(x,y,z)\)</p>
<p>\(\left \|   \nabla f({@point_x@},{@point_y@},{@point_z@}) \right \|=\sqrt{{x_1^2}+{y_1^2}+{z_1^2}}\) </p>
<p>[[input:ans10]] [[validation:ans10]][[feedback:prt10]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>
<p><p>Consider the following equation, we need to find the direction in which the maximum rate of change of the equation occurs and the maximum rate of change.</p>
<p>\(f(x,y,z)={@quiz_expr@}\) at \(({@point_x@},{@point_y@},{@point_z@})\)</p>
<p><b>Step \(1\):</b>  We need to compute \(\displaystyle\frac{d f(x,y,z)}{dx})=\nabla f_x, \frac{d f(x,y,z)}{dy})=\nabla f_y, \frac{d f(x,y,z)}{dz})=\nabla f_z\).</p>
<br>
<p>\(\nabla f_x={@function_1@}\)</p>
<p>\(\nabla f_y={@function_2@}\)</p>
<p>\(\nabla f_z={@function_2@}\)</p>
<br>
<p><b>Step \(2\):</b> Now, we can find the gradient  at the points \(({@point_x@},{@point_y@},{@point_z@})\) by substituting for the values of \(x,y,z\) respectively to the  above equation \(\displaystyle \nabla f_x,\nabla f_y,\nabla f_z\) b</p>
<p></p>
<p>\(\nabla f_x={@gradient1@}\)</p>
<p>\(\nabla f_y={@gradient2@}\)</p>
<p>\(\nabla f_z={@gradient3@}\)</p>
<br>
<p><b>Step \(3\):</b> The direction in which the maximum rate of change at the point in question is simply the gradient at  \(({@point_x@},{@point_y@},{@point_z@})\) which we found in the previous step. So, the direction in which the maximum rate of change of the function occurs is,</p>
<p>\(\nabla f_x(x-x_1)+\nabla f_y(y-y_1)+\nabla f_z(z-z_1)={@ta7@},{@ta8@},{@ta9@}\)</p>
<br>
<p><b>Step \(4\):</b> The maximum rate of change is simply the magnitude of the gradient in the previous step. So, the maximum rate of change of the function is,</p>
<p>\(\left \|   \nabla f({@point_x@},{@point_y@},{@point_z@}) \right \|=\sqrt{{x_1^2}+{y_1^2}+{z_1^2}}\)</p>
<p>\({@magnitude@}\) </p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
